## 1. isPunctuationOnly / new-bridge hunt

isPunctuationOnly (merchants.go:99-109) uses unicode.IsLetter/IsDigit, so it
is correctly Unicode-aware, not ASCII-only — confirmed by direct execution
(throwaway `go test -run` scratch file in the merchants package, deleted
after use; not part of the diff):

  "H & M"                -> "H M"          (was "H & M" pre-fix, 3 tokens)
  "H & M 45TH ST"         -> "H M 45TH ST"  (rich, safe)
  "7 - ELEVEN"            -> "ELEVEN"       (was "- ELEVEN" pre-fix; now
                                              degenerate/1-token, SAFER)
  "7-ELEVEN 4821 MAIN ST" -> "7-ELEVEN MAIN ST" (embedded hyphen untouched)
  "A & W RESTAURANT #123" -> "A W RESTAURANT" (rich, safe)
  "CASH $ WITHDRAWAL"     -> "CASH WITHDRAWAL"
  "ACME — COFFEE" / "ACME – COFFEE" / "ACME • COFFEE" -> "ACME COFFEE"
    (em-dash/en-dash/bullet all correctly classified as punctuation-only
    via Unicode categories, same as ASCII "-"/"*"/"#")
  "COFFEE €5"             -> "COFFEE €5"    (digit embedded in the token,
                                              survives — analogous to the
                                              existing AMZN order-ID case
                                              already logged in the P3
                                              verdict; can only cause a
                                              missed merge, not a bridge)

Confirmed a genuine NEW bridge class, not present pre-fix: dropping
standalone "&" shrinks ampersand-joined two-letter brand names ("H&M",
"A&W" when a bank/manual entry spaces them as "H & M") from a 3-token key
(old: {H,&,M}, guarded because old code never dropped "&" as a token) down
to a bare 2-token key of generic single-letter fragments ({H,M}) that IS
non-degenerate (size 2, passes the guard) and subset-merges into any
unrelated key containing both letters as standalone tokens. Reproduced
empirically:

  GroupKeys([Normalize("H & M"), Normalize("H M SUPERSTORE DOWNTOWN")])
    -> both map to "H M SUPERSTORE DOWNTOWN" (merged)
  GroupKeys([Normalize("A & W"), Normalize("A W ELECTRONICS DOWNTOWN")])
    -> both map to "A W ELECTRONICS DOWNTOWN" (merged)

Materiality assessment (judged honestly, not dismissed): this requires (a)
a raw description that is JUST the two-letter-ampersand name with no other
qualifier (store #, city, channel marker) — most real bank exports for
brands like H&M/A&W include a store number or city and land in the safe
4-token form shown above, but budget2 also carries user-editable
DisplayName, so a manually-typed "H & M" is plausible — AND (b) a second,
genuinely unrelated transaction elsewhere in the same user's data whose
normalized key independently contains BOTH bare single-letter tokens (e.g.
from address abbreviations "STE H"/"UNIT M"/compass "W"). Both conditions
co-occurring is a real but narrow coincidence, not a systemic bridge like
the {SQ,*} bug this task fixed (which affected every Square-processed
merchant sharing that exact processor format). No planted fixture, no
existing regression test, and no code path in this diff manufactures
condition (b) from condition (a) alone. This is the same class of risk the
2-token-floor subset-merge design already accepts by construction (P1,
already PASSED: "ACME COFFEE" (2 meaningful-word tokens) merging into any
superset containing both words is the intended behavior) — punctuation-drop
just extends the token-shrinking effect to single-letter fragments, which
have a higher (but still low in practice) coincidence probability than
whole words. No test in merchants_test.go exercises ampersand-joined names
at all (grep confirms zero "&" cases). Judgment: real, worth a fast
follow-up (e.g. also excluding single-character alpha tokens from
counting toward the subset-merge "≥2 tokens" floor, mirroring how P1's
spaced-asterisk nit became this very P5 fix) — but NOT a regression against
P5's stated acceptance criteria (SQ-bridge closed; verified below), and not
exploitable by any current fixture/test data. Flagging as a material,
non-blocking finding, not a defect that fails this task.

{SQ,*} fix itself verified correct: TestGroupKeys_SpacedAsteriskNoLongerBridges
(merchants_test.go:356-397) — traced by hand and matches Normalize output;
"SQ * COFFEE SHOP"/"SQ * PIZZA PLACE" -> {SQ,COFFEE,SHOP}/{SQ,PIZZA,PLACE}
(no merge), and the bare "SQ *" row -> "SQ" (1 token, degenerate, exact-
equality only) no longer bridges. Confirms the specific bug named in the
Ruling is closed.

## 2. Downstream ripple on anomalies (P2)

anomalies.go is untouched by P5 (not in .swarm/manifests/P5.1.files) but
consumes merchants.GroupTransactions transitively, so its qualifying-group
(n>=4) status for rule (a) exclusion can shift purely from Normalize's new
behavior. Two opposing effects reasoned through: (i) closing {SQ,*}-style
over-merging can only SHRINK previously-inflated groups (fewer false n>=4
qualifications, correcting toward truth); (ii) the ampersand-shrinkage
effect from §1 could in principle GROW a group across n=4, pulling those
transactions out of the category baseline and into merchant-only scoring —
an intended consequence of using the shared, ruled Normalize, not a bug,
since rule (a) is explicit by design.

Ran the full anomalies suite against the new merchants package:
`go test ./internal/services/anomalies/... -race -count=2` -> PASS, no
race reports. TestDetect_PlantedFixture_FlagsFiveWithAtMostOneFalsePositive
(the P2 acceptance gate: 5/5 planted anomalies flagged, <=1 FP) still
passes, confirming the planted fixture's merchant grouping was not
disrupted by the P5 Normalize change. No planted fixture data contains
punctuation-only or ampersand-joined tokens, so this is direct evidence for
the fixture only; real-data risk is the same narrow ampersand-shrinkage
case from §1, carried here as "could flip a merchant group's n>=4 status
and reroute its transactions from category-baseline to merchant-only MAD
scoring" — plausible but requires the same narrow real-world coincidence,
not something this diff's own tests can or need to pin.

## 3. DisplayLabel factoring — behavior identity and collision safety

merchants.DisplayLabel(group []models.Transaction) (merchants.go:232-268)
picks the most-frequent raw DisplayName-or-Description among the group
(ties broken by first occurrence), then lowercases+trims. Godoc present
and explicit about why this must be used instead of the canonical
uppercase-normalized key (merchants.go:238-241).

Could not git-diff against the pre-factoring handlers.go helper (untracked,
no history), so verified two ways instead:
  a) All pre-existing insights recurring-detection tests that assert exact
     `.Description`/label strings (TestDetectRecurringPayments_
     FuzzyMergedVendor "Lucid", _AmountBasedGrouping, _ExactMatchStillWorks,
     _LongHistoryPattern, etc. — handlers_test.go) still pass under
     `-race -count=2`, which is strong behavioral-identity evidence for
     real fixture inputs since these tests were written and passing
     against the pre-factoring implementation per the P3 verdict.
  b) Re-derived the P3 checker's collision-impossibility proof ("two
     different canonical groups cannot produce an identical display
     label") against the NEW, more complex mode-based DisplayLabel, since
     that proof was written for a simpler `ToLower(TrimSpace(raw))` helper
     and the richer majority-vote logic is not obviously covered by it.
     DisplayLabel always returns a label copied verbatim from one member
     transaction's own raw field (never synthesized). If two DIFFERENT
     (unmerged) canonical clusters produced lower-trim-equal winning
     labels, their two owning raw strings would be lower-trim-equal to
     each other, and Normalize-equality is coarser than (implied by)
     lower-trim-equality (Normalize also uppercases, collapses internal
     whitespace, and drops digit/punctuation tokens — a superset of
     lower+trim). So those two raw strings would already produce identical
     GroupTransactions rawKeys, forcing them into ONE shared canonical
     cluster via the unconditional a==b branch in shouldMerge
     (merchants.go:214-216) before DisplayLabel is ever computed —
     contradicting the premise that they were separate clusters. Collision
     across genuinely distinct clusters remains structurally impossible
     under the factored implementation, not just the original simpler one.

Nit (test hygiene, non-blocking): no direct unit test for DisplayLabel
exists in merchants_test.go (grep confirms zero matches) — its tie-break
logic (majority count, first-seen tie-break) is only exercised indirectly
through pricecreep/handlers integration tests, all of which pass, but a
focused table test (e.g. a 2-way count tie resolved by first occurrence)
would pin the contract directly rather than through call-site behavior.

Carried-forward, not new: DisplayLabel's `if raw == ""` fallback (not
TrimSpace-aware) — already flagged as a low-materiality nit in the P1
verdict for the pre-factoring GroupTransactions code; unchanged by the
factoring, still low materiality (requires a whitespace-only DisplayName).

## 4. Same-day tie-break

pricecreep.go:97-109 sorts with `sort.SliceStable` and an explicit
secondary key: `sorted[i].Hash < sorted[j].Hash` when dates are equal —
confirmed by direct read, not inferred. TestDetect_SameDayTieBrokenByHash
(pricecreep_test.go:330-380) plants a 6-row group where rows at sorted
indices 2 and 3 (exactly the first-3/last-3 boundary at the minimum
occurrence count) share one date, and asserts identical FirstAmount/
CurrentAmount across three input orderings (forward/shuffled/reverse) —
this does genuinely straddle the boundary, not an interior tie that
wouldn't affect the windows.

Empirically flipped the comparator to reproduce the OLD (pre-fix,
Date-only, no Hash key) behavior via a throwaway scratch test file in the
pricecreep package (not part of the diff; created, run 5x, then deleted —
did not modify pricecreep.go itself, which is out of scope for this
checker to edit). Result: the old comparator IS order-dependent on this
exact fixture, reproducibly across 5 runs:
  forward:            firstMedian=60,  lastMedian=500
  shuffled / reverse:  firstMedian=100, lastMedian=500
confirming (1) the pre-fix tie-break bug was real and would have made this
new test flaky/order-dependent under the old sort, and (2) the new
Hash-ascending secondary key is both present and load-bearing — it is not
a no-op fix.

## 5. Cross-package filter alignment (Ruling item 3)

pricecreep.go:59-64,80-84 now filters `t.TransactionType == models.Outflow
&& t.Amount < 0`, matching anomalies.go:161's filter exactly (both
verified by direct read). TestDetect_ExpensesOnly, _OutflowPositiveAmount
Excluded, and _MistaggedIncomeExcluded (pricecreep_test.go:242-314) pin
this. The P3-flagged inconsistency (pricecreep previously used a bare
`Amount < 0`) is closed.

