## Summary

No material defect against P5's stated acceptance criteria: the {SQ,*}
bridge is closed and verified by hand-traced test plus direct execution;
the anomalies ripple was checked both by running its full suite (green,
5/5 planted anomalies + <=1 FP intact) and by reasoning through the
real-data risk; DisplayLabel factoring is behavior-identical on every
existing assertion and its collision-safety proof was independently
re-derived (not just assumed carried-over) for the richer implementation;
the same-day tie-break is real, present, and empirically shown to fix a
genuine order-dependence bug, on a test that actually straddles the
window boundary; the pricecreep/anomalies filter mismatch is closed.

One material-but-narrow finding surfaced by directly hunting the attack
surface the task asked about: dropping standalone punctuation tokens
(specifically "&") can shrink ampersand-joined two-letter brand names into
a bare, generic 2-token key capable of bridging into unrelated data that
happens to contain both single letters as standalone tokens (reproduced
empirically for "H & M" and "A & W"). This is real and not covered by any
current test, but is low-probability in practice, requires two independent
coincidences neither of which any current fixture or plausible common bank
export format produces, and is an extension of an already-accepted design
tradeoff (the 2-token subset-merge floor) rather than a regression against
P5's own acceptance criteria. Recommend a fast follow-up ruling (e.g.
exclude single-character alpha tokens from the subset-merge token count,
the same pattern by which this task itself originated from a P3 nit) but
it does not block P5 acceptance.
